   "subject": "Interpreting hotspots in a shared spatial field from a joint model with two species",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Hi All,</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"></span><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<u></u><u></u>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">We are using a joint model with a shared spatial field to determine the common spatial trends\r\nfor two different species based on work put forward in Sadykova et al. (2017) - <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442468951000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3h3VdxWzCg-QHPunZgqCFk\"><span style=\"color:#6611cc\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ece3.3081</span></a>.  In\r\nthe paper, they create a joint model that has a shared spatial field for pairs\r\nof different species (using the copy function) in order to identify areas of\r\ncommon spatial trends.  The full code for the joint spatial model is\r\navailable from the authors here under &quot;Joint Spatial Models&quot;: <u></u><u></u></span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi=10.1002%2Fece3.3081&amp;file=ece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi%3D10.1002%252Fece3.3081%26file%3Dece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792442468951000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0zV3QF7r5hTVCphmIX2do_\"><span style=\"color:#6611cc\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/action/downloadSupplement?doi=10.1002%2Fece3.3081&amp;file=ece33081-sup-0002-AppendixS2.txt</span></a></span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">The shared spatial field part of the model formula and INLA call\r\nare below designating a shared spatial field for 3 different species using the\r\ncopy function (we are only using two species in our case):</span><u></u><u></u><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"> <u></u><u></u></span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:blue;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"> </span><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"> f(i.spat1, model=&quot;bym&quot;, graph = &quot;graph2.dat&quot;, hyper= param.spat01) +</span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u>  f(i.spat2, copy=&quot;i.spat1&quot;, fixed=F) +</span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u>  f(i.spat3, copy=&quot;i.spat1&quot;, fixed=F)</span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u> </span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">  result.j1 &lt;- inla(formula,family=c(&quot;gamma&quot;,&quot;gamma&quot;,&quot;gamma&quot;), data=data, <u></u><u></u></span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">               control.compute=list(dic=T), <u></u><u></u></span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">               control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE), verbose=TRUE,<u></u><u></u></span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">               control.fixed = list(expand.factor.strategy = &quot;inla&quot;)) <u></u><u></u></span><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><u></u> <u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><u></u> <u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Several environmental covariates are also included in the model formula to account for habitat preferences. </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">The resulting spatial field is then plotted to identify hotspots, described in the paper a</span><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">s &quot;</span>residual spatial autocorrelation unexplained by covariates&quot; (Figure 3 from the paper below, pairs of different species assumed to be competitors):</font></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><u></u> <u></u><u></u><u></u></pre><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/r-inla-discussion-group/attach/ea6a551ebfe75/maps.jpg?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"maps.jpg\" width=\"320\" height=\"162\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\"></p><div><br></div><div><br></div><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">We have been able to plot similar figures for the spatial field (after including covariates as well) and have found analogous hot spots. However, I was just hoping for some ideas on exactly how these spatial dependencies could be interpreted. </span> <u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Sadykova et al. (2017) in section 4.2.3 of their paper describe hot spot areas as &quot;high activity areas of the coupled species&quot;.  However, I am assuming that shared spatial dependence (after taking environmental covariates into account) could also be in areas where there is low &quot;activity&quot; in terms of the absolute numbers of each species?  That is, hotspots on the spatial field would not necessarily correlate with areas where there is the highest number of either species. </span><u></u><u></u></pre><pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Do these hotspots tell us anything about the interaction between these two species at these locations? </span> <u></u><u></u></pre>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">In the case of a spatial field for a single response variable,\r\nZuur et al. 2017 (Spatial, Temporal and Spatial-Temporal Ecological Data\r\nAnalysis with R-INLA) describe an example of bird nests:</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u><u></u> <u></u></span><u></u><u></u></pre>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">“A value of µ<sub>i</sub> = 0.8 at a nest in one of these\r\nhotspots has an expected number of fledged chicks that are around exp(0.8) = 2\r\nlarger than at a nest not in such a hotspot.” – page 318</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<pre style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u><u></u> <u></u></span><u></u><u></u></pre>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Is there a way to characterize a shared spatial field in a similar\r\nmanner? <u></u><u></u> Could the posterior means of a shared spatial\r\nfield also be described in this type of quantitative way when the field\r\nrepresents the shared spatial dependence between both of the species?</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Many thanks for the help!</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Paul </span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;line-height:107%;font-family:Arial,sans-serif\"><u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
